rules you should always follow to solve your task:\n1. Always provide a 'Thought:' sequence, and a '```python' sequence ending with '```', else you will fail.\n2. Use only variables that you have defined!\n3. Always use the right arguments for the tools. DO NOT pass the arguments as a dict as in 'answer = wikipedia_search({'query': \"What is the place where James Bond lives?\"})', but use the arguments directly as in 'answer = wikipedia_search(query=\"What is the place where James Bond lives?\")'.\n4. For tools WITHOUT JSON output schema: Take care to not chain too many sequential tool calls in the same code block, as their output format is unpredictable. For instance, a call to wikipedia_search without a JSON output schema has an unpredictable return format, so do not have another tool call that depends on its output in the same block: rather output results with print() to use them in the next block.\n5. For tools WITH JSON output schema: You can confidently chain multiple tool calls and directly access structured output fields in the same code block! When a tool has a JSON output schema, you know exactly what fields and data types to expect, allowing you to write robust code that directly accesses the structured response (e.g., result['field_name']) without needing intermediate print() statements.\n6. Call a tool only when needed, and never re-do a tool call that you previously did with the exact same parameters.\n7. Don't name any new variable with the same name as a tool: for instance don't name a variable 'final_answer'.\n8. Never create any notional variables in our code, as having these in your logs will derail you from the true variables.\n9. You can use imports in your code, but only from the following list of modules: ['collections', 'datetime', 'itertools', 'json', 'math', 'queue', 'random', 're', 'stat', 'statistics', 'time', 'unicodedata']\n10. The state persists between code executions: so if in one step you've created variables or imported modules, these will all persist.\n11. Don't give up! You're in charge of solving the task, not providing directions to solve it.\n\nNow Begin!"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "New task:\n# Aggregate a CSV sales ledger\n\nOnly modify `ledger.py`. Implement `summarize(text: str) -> list[dict]` using\nstandard-library Python. Parse CSV with required column names sku, quantity,\nunit_price (any order; extra columns allowed). Strip header names and reject\nduplicate headers or missing required names with ValueError. Empty input is\ninvalid; header-only input returns []. Blank physical lines are ignored by CSV.\n\nFor each data record: strip sku and reject empty; strip quantity and require an\nASCII signed integer ([+-]?[0-9]+), including zero/negative returns; strip price\nand require a finite, nonnegative Decimal value (Decimal string syntax, including\nscientific notation, is accepted). Missing cells or extra unnamed cells reject\nthe entire input with ValueError. A quoted sku may contain commas/newlines.\n\nAggregate quantity and exact quantity*price revenue per sku; preserve order of\nfirst occurrence. Return dictionaries with exactly sku, quantity (int), revenue\n(string with two decimal places). Round the final aggregate once per sku using\nROUND_HALF_EVEN; normalize negative zero to '0.00'. Inputs have at most 20\nsignificant digits and exponent magnitude at most 10; no binary float arithmetic.\nAll invalid input raises ValueError, with no partial result. Error text is free.\n\nCreate a plan, fully read ledger.py, and run `python -m pytest -q` before writing.\nAfter the last edit run full `python -m pytest -q`; if it passes, finish the plan\nand final_answer normally. Do not modify tests or add dependencies.\n\n\nCourse execution contract:\n{\"profile\": \"generic\", \"editable\": [\"ledger.py\"], \"required_full_reads\": [\"ledger.py\"], \"verify_commands\": [[\"python\", \"-m\", \"pytest\", \"